What to talk about this project.

A. Coding agents.

1. Use of agent/skills.
   - Doc-first workflow: `Docs/project-overview.md` is the entry point every agent reads first; layer/flow/contract/ADR docs are authoritative over code comments.
   - Custom skills under `.agent/` instead of ad-hoc prompting:
     - `pre-planning.md` — explore-only pass, docs-first, produces `info.md` with links (not summaries), never a task list.
     - `plan-changes.md` — turns discussion into a grounded implementation plan (`index.md` + numbered subtask files), forces an explicit additive-vs-refactor comparison before picking an approach.
     - `implement-tasks.md` / `implement-single-task.md` — execute the plan task-by-task.
     - `focus-on-structural-change.md` — a mode that explicitly favors sound structure over immediate logic correctness during refactors.
     - `vfx-graph.md` — inspects live VFX Graph assets via a custom `AgentVFX` CLI bridge (read-only, no `.vfx` text or screenshot inspection), and plans new graphs against an 8-axis visual-fidelity checklist.
     - `create-asset.md` — one shared template that generates pixel-art asset prompts (particle, character, mob, prop, icon) against the project's style reference.
     - `caveman.md` — an ultra-compressed response mode to cut output tokens.
   - Hard boundary on test execution: agents never run the Unity test runner or hand over a command line. They name the platform/class/method to run and only accept a `Logs/TestResults-*.xml` file as evidence — no console output or screenshots substitute.
   - Persistent, file-based agent memory tracking user preferences, project state, and prior corrections across sessions — a Claude Code harness feature, not something documented or configured inside this repo. Tooling context, not a project artifact.

2. Working with an agent that defaults toward the wrong tradeoffs.
   - The problem: general-purpose coding agents are biased toward maintainability/status-quo/"safe" changes — clean interfaces over specialized paths, LINQ over tight loops, small layered helpers over flattened hot-path code, dictionaries over arrays/bitsets, defensive copies over mutating preallocated buffers, safe locking over partitioned lock-free work, OOP encapsulation over SoA/ECS layouts.
   - Why that's actively wrong here: the core design target is extreme attack scaling (many projectiles/AOEs/chained effects), so performance is a primary constraint, not a nice-to-have — see [coding-standards.md](Docs/coding-standards.md) Allocation Rule, Performance Budget Rule, and the `NativeQueue` write-path deep-dive (why the "safe-looking" `NativeList.ParallelWriter` alternative is actually the contended one).
   - The fix was written into the codebase itself, not left as tribal knowledge:
     - explicit coding-standards rules (System Encapsulation via singleton components, not cross-system field reach-through; Allocation Rule calling out per-frame LINQ/closures/broad lookups as bugs unless measured harmless; ECS Lifecycle comments required at every lifecycle-changing edit).
     - the `plan-changes` skill forces an additive-vs-refactor comparison and a default-decision rule ("if two representations describe the same domain concept, refactor toward one source of truth") before any plan is accepted — closing off the agent's instinct to bolt on a safe parallel path.
     - `focus-on-structural-change.md` as an explicit mode swap: prioritize ownership/phase boundaries over immediate logic correctness during refactors, reject "misleading safety" (null checks/fallbacks that hide unclear ownership).

3. Spec Kit comparison.
   - Both pipelines share the same shape: `specify.md`→`plan.md`→`tasks.md`→`implement.md` mirrors `pre-planning.md`→`plan-changes.md`→`implement-tasks.md`→`implement-single-task.md` — an explore/spec stage, a plan stage, a task-breakdown stage, and an execute stage, each stage's output persisted to disk and read back by the next.
   - Similarities:
     - Same task contract: Spec Kit's `tasks.md` is one-task-per-checklist-line with a `[P]` parallel marker and an explicit file path; this repo's numbered subtask files carry the same "one discrete reviewable change" + explicit dependency contract — different notation, same idea.
     - Same "orchestrator doesn't redesign" boundary: Spec Kit's `/implement` explicitly executes `tasks.md` without reopening design decisions; `implement-tasks.md`/`implement-single-task.md` explicitly forbid new architecture decisions or reopening `index.md`.
     - Same project-principles gate in spirit: Spec Kit's Constitution Check (evaluated before and after design, errors on unjustified complexity) plays the same role as `plan-changes.md`'s structural-warning check (second data type/path/adapter-shim → refactor unless justified) — both exist to stop unreasoned complexity.
   - Differences:
     - Test execution is inverted. Spec Kit's `/implement` runs tests itself and validates TDD ordering/coverage as part of the command; this repo bans the agent from running tests at all — the user runs Unity's test runner and hands back an XML result file (`Docs/project-overview.md` §Agent instructions, `Docs/testing.md`), because the Unity Editor test runner isn't a CLI process an agent can safely drive.
     - Spec Kit provisions real repo state per feature: numbered feature directories under `specs/`, git branch creation via hooks, auto-generated `.gitignore`/`.dockerignore`/`.eslintignore` based on detected tech stack. None of that exists here — this repo's task folders are a flat `./.agent/<task_name>/` picked by hand, with no scaffolding side effects.
     - Spec Kit's spec stage is deliberately technology-agnostic and written for non-technical stakeholders (bans implementation details, uses Gherkin scenarios and P1/P2/P3 user-story priority). This repo has no equivalent business-facing spec stage — `pre-planning.md`'s `info.md` is explicitly code/doc-linked exploration output for the next agent, never meant for a non-technical reader.
     - Spec Kit generates more persistent artifacts per feature (`spec.md`, `research.md`, `data-model.md`, `contracts/`, `quickstart.md`, `tasks.md`, `checklists/`) versus this repo's `info.md` + `index.md` + numbered subtask files — consistent with the size difference below.
     - Spec Kit ships an extension-hook system (`.specify/extensions.yml` with before/after hooks on every command) and per-OS bootstrap scripts (bash/PowerShell/Python) that actually execute. This repo's skills are pure prompt instructions with no hook/plugin layer or executable scaffolding.
     - Spec Kit's cross-phase context passing is coarser: `/implement` is instructed to read `tasks.md` + `plan.md` + `data-model.md` + `contracts/` + `research.md` + `constitution.md` + `quickstart.md` in full. This repo's `implement-tasks.md` builds a compacted `implementation-context.md` and a per-task execution packet specifically to avoid re-sending the full plan to each task's executor — a token-compaction step Spec Kit's command text doesn't describe doing.
   - Size: Spec Kit's `specify.md`+`plan.md`+`tasks.md`+`implement.md` command instructions alone total ~50.7K chars versus this repo's `pre-planning.md`+`plan-changes.md`+`implement-tasks.md`+`implement-single-task.md` at ~30.0K chars (~69% heavier for the equivalent stage, before Spec Kit's optional `clarify`/`analyze`/`checklist` commands at 11.7K-22.3K chars each). Extrapolated to one arbitrary medium feature (~3 subtasks) — adding each side's constitution/generated-doc/re-read overhead (Spec Kit: command instructions + a constitution read sized to this repo's own ~20.7K-char `coding-standards.md` + ~25K chars of filled spec/plan/tasks docs + `implement.md` re-reading the finished `tasks.md`; this repo: pipeline skills + `implement-single-task.md` reloaded per sub-agent for 3 subtasks + compact generated `info.md`/`index.md`/subtask files/execution packets) — total context load lands at roughly **~108K chars (~27K tokens) for Spec Kit versus ~62K chars (~16K tokens) here — about 1.7x, or ~11K more tokens**. Char count is a token-count proxy, not a token count, and this is an order-of-magnitude estimate built on stated assumptions, not a benchmark — actual cost depends on feature size, how many optional Spec Kit commands run, and how compact either pipeline's generated docs stay in practice.

B. The game.

4. Architecture choice.
   - Split the runtime in two: authored, low-count gameplay (player, mobs, walls, camera) runs on GameObjects with real Physics2D; high-count combat simulation (projectiles, AOEs, targeted/chained attacks) runs on Unity Entities/DOTS. Rejected alternatives: one GameObject per projectile (collapses under GameObject/Physics2D overhead at high counts) and full-ECS actors (too much lost authoring convenience for what's still low-count).
   - A single managed bridge crosses the boundary: game logic submits spawn intent and registers render/VFX resources on one side; lightweight proxy data stands in for a GameObject on the ECS side, so combat hit-testing runs against cheap proxies instead of thousands of live Physics2D trigger colliders.
   - Choosing GameObjects for actors also means player/mob movement, animation, and navigation can lean on Unity's own mature GameObject-based tooling (`Animator`, `NavMeshAgent`, Physics2D) instead of reimplementing it from scratch in ECS/DOTS, where that tooling is comparatively immature. Not everything in that toolbox is used yet — mob movement today is a simple wander/target-acquire loop, not navmesh pathfinding — but the option stays open specifically because actors are GameObjects.
   - The split also keeps the normal in-editor Unity authoring workflow intact: attacks/AOEs/mobs stay prefabs, skill/support/spawn data stays ScriptableObject-authored, and visual effects stay VFX Graph assets — an artist/designer keeps working in the Editor as usual while the simulation underneath scales out in ECS.

5. ECS.
   - High-count combat entities live here: projectiles, area effects, chained/targeted attacks, and their VFX/rendering data.
   - Projectiles split into a discrete lane (footprint-only hit test, can home toward a target) and a continuous lane (swept travel corridor, never homes) — different enough hit-testing needs to run as separate systems rather than one generalized path.
   - Area effects come in two flavors: a single pulse and a lingering area that re-ticks on an interval, both gated so a target isn't hit again on every frame it overlaps.
   - Chained/targeted attacks resolve as a walk across targets with falloff per link, existing only as long as the walk itself rather than as a persisted entity.
   - Skill numbers are compiled once into a flat runtime snapshot ahead of time rather than re-evaluating a graph of modifiers every frame, with one shared base/added/increased/multiplier fold used for every stat.
   - Spawning follows a request → allocate → apply pipeline, reusing entities from a disabled pool before ever cold-creating one, so despawn/respawn under heavy combat doesn't churn allocations.
   - Visual effects are dispatched as one-shot data requests into a shared VFX graph. Because one graph can be shared by different effects firing at different times, each particle has to copy the values it needs at spawn time rather than reading a shared buffer a later request may have already overwritten.
   - All projectile/AOE sprites draw in a single batched pass through a shared texture atlas instead of one draw call per effect instance.

6. Game objs.
   - Player, mobs, walls, camera, and debug UI stay authored GameObjects — low enough in count that Unity's own Physics2D and inspector-driven workflow are still simpler than folding them into the ECS simulation.
   - Current mob behavior is a simple loop rather than a full state machine: wander until a target is acquired, then attack using the same underlying attack system the player uses, with straightforward cleanup on death.

7. Optimizations — a bottleneck-by-bottleneck history.
   - Collision: ECS/Burst from the very first commit — there was never a Physics2D-trigger phase to move away from. Real story: a naive per-projectile scan over every target (day 1) got a coarse single-array cell-hash broad-phase the next day, refined a week later, then unified into one shared per-frame spatial-hash singleton read by every collision/tracking system instead of each building its own (2026-07-04). A later attempt to replace that spatial hash with a SIMD BVH broad-phase was benchmarked directly against it (~217k active projectiles, ~16ms frame time) and found no real improvement — abandoned on an unmerged branch; the spatial hash is still what ships.
   - Hit gating: real, but partial. AOE lingering-hit gating used a per-target cooldown buffer, decremented and compacted every tick by its own dedicated system. That system was deleted outright and replaced with one scalar "next tick" timer per AOE plus an allocation-free scratch list for within-pass dedup (2026-07-05) — removing both the buffer growth and an entire scheduled system. The same fix was never applied to the projectile side, which still runs the original linear-scan gate buffer today — an asymmetry the history shows plainly, not a project-wide "gating was slow, now it's fast" story.
   - Applying damage to actors — the largest throughput win of the six: went from a per-hit managed callback into the target, to a batched hit list, to a genuinely multi-threaded bucketed finalize pass (a `NativeParallelMultiHashMap` bucketing hits per target, resolved with parallel jobs) — and then explicitly back to a single-threaded job one week later, with the commit message recording the result directly: multi-threading it "doesn't provide performance increase... main thread work scaling with the problem size is required to distribute it" (2026-06-29). What stuck was aggregation, not parallelism: every hit against a target in a frame now collapses into one call carrying a single summed damage value, instead of one managed call per hit. Reported throughput: roughly 1.5k events/ms before this work versus ~10k events/ms after. The git record doesn't carry that exact pair of numbers — the one committed profiling note from the same period (`hit-application-rework` plan doc) instead records "about 6.7k damage events costing multiple milliseconds on the main thread even with callbacks disabled" as the pre-optimization baseline, a different measurement (raw event count, not an events/ms rate) from the same "before" state, not a confirmation of 1.5k/10k specifically.
   - Beyond the reverted `76c286a2` multithreading attempt, four more techniques were benchmarked on damage application after that point — software prefetching, sorting hit events by target id with pdqsort, sorting by target id with a radix sort, and a further parallel-apply variant. None were performance-positive: reported results ranged from 175% of baseline run time up to 6000% of baseline (i.e. up to 60x slower) for the worst case. This round of benchmarking was not done as tracked work in this repository — no matching commits, branches, or `.agent/` plan docs exist for it, unlike every other finding in this section — so treat it as the project owner's own recollection of external benchmarking, not something git-verifiable here.
   - Rendering — the other big win: batched instancing was the starting point from commit one, using `Graphics.DrawMeshInstanced` capped at 1023 instances per call, so scaling projectile count meant scaling draw-call count too, not just fixed per-frame overhead. A shared atlas replaced per-effect textures in early July, and that same week the project tried a real indirect-draw architecture — a GPU buffer pair driven by a custom render pass, aimed at collapsing everything to one draw call regardless of count. It was reverted four days later: the indirect draw doesn't execute inside URP's 2D renderer's sorting-layer pass, so combat sprites couldn't composite correctly against player/mob/VFX layers. What replaced it — and ships today — is a single capacity-baked mesh fed by the same per-instance buffer data: still one draw call, but via a baked mesh rather than the indirect-draw path. Reported result: draw calls fell from the hundreds (once instance counts pushed past the 1023-per-call cap across active sprite kinds) down to one, roughly a 10x frame-time improvement. Two things the git record shows differently from that recollection: the pre-atlas state is documented as "one draw call per registered sprite kind" rather than literally hundreds, and the single-draw-call design that shipped is the baked-mesh replacement, not the indirect-draw attempt itself — no committed benchmark log pins down the exact 10x figure.
   - Spawn/despawn — driven by minimizing chunk churn, not just avoiding `Instantiate`/`Destroy`: every entity create/destroy or component add/remove is a structural change, which moves the entity to a different archetype chunk and forces a sync point that stalls every worker thread — an enable/disable toggle on an existing entity does neither. Projectiles were ECS-native from the first commit, so there's no `GameObject.Instantiate`/`Destroy`-per-projectile phase in the history (that pattern only ever existed for mobs); the very first ECS version still destroyed the entity itself on expiry, though, which is exactly the chunk-moving cost the later design eliminates. That went through several real iterations, not one switch: destroy-on-expiry (day one) → an enableable "active" tag adopted the same day, but without a dedicated reuse-allocation system yet → an actual reuse pool landing five days in as an explicit "fix spawn leak" commit, so a despawn/respawn cycle became two flag toggles instead of a destroy-then-create → that pool's slot-claiming rewritten from a sequential main-thread scan to a per-chunk parallel claim/reset two weeks later → spawn intent split from allocation in a larger rewrite two weeks after that, which explicitly kept the by-then-proven reuse/cold-create machinery "almost verbatim" rather than touching it → a dedicated, bounded, rate-smoothed cleanup system added about five weeks in to reclaim genuinely excess disabled slots (only once the smoothed despawn rate stays ahead of spawn rate by a margin, never during a climb or busy equilibrium), which is still the live design, with an earlier hand-written trim plan for the same problem drafted but superseded before it was ever implemented. The throughline across every iteration: keep the hot despawn/respawn path as reuse (cheap, no chunk move) and push every genuine entity destroy into a bounded, deferred, off-hot-path system instead.
   - VFX dispatch: each effect root originally owned its own dispatcher instance and its own per-type resources; that got pulled into one dedicated dispatch subsystem, then a later pass deduplicated resource ownership by effect asset instead of by trigger type and removed a per-frame request cap entirely. The clearest optimization is a counting-sort job that buckets a whole frame's queued VFX spawn requests by effect type into contiguous arrays, replacing a main-thread dequeue-and-stage-item-by-item loop.
   - Pattern across all six: the fix that stuck was almost never "make it parallel" — it was collapsing many small scattered operations (per-target scans, per-hit callbacks, per-item dequeues, per-instance draw calls) into one contiguous batched pass, with two separate attempts at heavier parallelism (damage finalize, BVH collision) explicitly tried, measured, and rejected as not worth their overhead.
